# Atlas OSS install: configuration (STORY-oss-10). Copy to .env in this directory and edit:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# This file holds NO SECRET and must never hold one. Secrets are files under ./secrets/, made by
# ../../scripts/init-secrets.sh and passed as compose `secrets:`; ATLAS_REQUIRE_SECRET_FILES=1
# below makes atlas-api and atlas-mcp refuse a secret given as a plain variable. .env is read
# twice: by compose for the ${...} in docker-compose.yml, and by atlas-api and atlas-mcp as
# their `env_file:`, so what is set here is what the processes see.
#
# The values below are the WORK INSTALL's (ADR 0041 §0: project RBAC, private by default,
# personal features off, secrets as files only). The code's own defaults are the homelab's;
# this file is where an install opts in. Every variable the code reads is named here, set or
# commented (tools/ci/check_env_example.py fails the build when one is missing).
#
# The optional MCP gateway (STORY-oss-15) has its own section below, off unless its profile
# is started. OIDC login and IdP groups (STORY-oss-13) are in their own section below,
# commented until the IdP app registration exists: local accounts are the login until
# you turn it on.

# ============================================================================================
# Names and the front door
# ============================================================================================

# The DNS name (or IP address) people and agents use, without scheme or port. atlas-tls answers
# for this name only, and init-secrets.sh puts it in the server certificate.
ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST=atlas.example.com

# The port the TLS ingress is published on, and the interface. 443 on every interface by
# default: this is the install's one door and it is HTTPS. Use 127.0.0.1 to keep it local.
ATLAS_TLS_PORT=443
ATLAS_TLS_BIND=0.0.0.0

# The one absolute URL Atlas is reached on: https://ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST, plus :ATLAS_TLS_PORT when
# that is not 443. The login origins and the hosts on which a Bearer token is honoured are
# derived from it. Keep it in step with the two values above.
ATLAS_PUBLIC_URL=https://atlas.example.com

# Derived from ATLAS_PUBLIC_URL while unset; set them only to widen or narrow that.
# ATLAS_LOGIN_ORIGINS=https://atlas.example.com
# ATLAS_BEARER_HOSTS=atlas.example.com,atlas.example.com:443

# The Host headers atlas-mcp answers on /mcp (DNS-rebinding protection; any other Host is a
# 421). Clients arrive through atlas-tls with the public name; loopback serves the container's
# own health probe. Add ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST:ATLAS_TLS_PORT when the port is not 443.
# atlas-mcp:9444 is the gateway profile's hop (atlas-tls sets that Host on it); harmless
# without the profile, required with it.
ATLAS_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=atlas.example.com,atlas.example.com:443,localhost:*,127.0.0.1:*,atlas-mcp:9444
# Checked only when a request sends an Origin header (browsers do; MCP clients do not).
ATLAS_MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://atlas.example.com

# The subnet of the internal `origin` network (atlas-tls and atlas-api only). Pinned so that
# atlas-api believes the client address atlas-tls reports from exactly that network
# (ATLAS_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS is set from it in the compose file). Change it only if it collides
# with a network on this host; `docker compose up` then fails loudly, never silently.
ATLAS_ORIGIN_SUBNET=172.30.255.0/24

# The mailbox the privacy page names, and the product's display name.
ATLAS_PRIVACY_CONTACT=privacy@example.com
ATLAS_PRODUCT_NAME=Atlas

# Where the privacy page says the data is hosted, as plain text. Describe your own hosting:
# the code's default describes a different install, so leaving it unset states something
# false about yours.
ATLAS_PRIVACY_HOSTING=One PostgreSQL database on this organisation's own server. It is not copied to a third party.

# ============================================================================================
# TLS (STORY-oss-24). Every hop that carries a token is HTTPS with the certificate verified.
# ============================================================================================
#
# Where the public certificate comes from:
#   files            ./secrets/tls/server.crt + server.key (default). init-secrets.sh makes them
#                    with init-certs.sh from a throwaway install CA when there is no PKI; with
#                    your enterprise PKI, put its files there instead BEFORE running it:
#                      ./secrets/tls/server.crt   full chain, SANs: ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST and atlas-api
#                      ./secrets/tls/server.key   its key
#                      ./secrets/tls/ca.crt       your root (atlas-mcp trusts exactly this file)
#   internal         Caddy's own CA for the public door (clients trust its root, in the
#                    atlas-tls-data volume at /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt)
#   acme-cloudflare  Let's Encrypt by DNS-01; the Cloudflare token in the file named below
# atlas-mcp's own hop (https://atlas-api:9443) always uses ./secrets/tls/server.crt, checked
# against ./secrets/tls/ca.crt.
ATLAS_TLS_MODE=files
# acme-cloudflare only: the PATH of a file holding the token, never the token.
# ATLAS_TLS_CF_TOKEN_FILE=./secrets/cf_dns_api_token

# The mTLS leg on atlas-mcp's hop to the API: off (default) | require. With require, atlas-tls
# refuses a connection to atlas-api:9443 without a client certificate issued by the CA in
# ATLAS_TLS_CLIENT_CA_FILE (default ./secrets/tls/client-ca.crt), and atlas-mcp presents its
# own: uncomment the two ATLAS_MCP_CLIENT_* lines together with this one.
ATLAS_TLS_CLIENT_AUTH=off
# ATLAS_TLS_CLIENT_CA_FILE=./secrets/tls/client-ca.crt
# ATLAS_MCP_CLIENT_CERT_FILE=/run/secrets/mcp_client_cert
# ATLAS_MCP_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/mcp_client_key

# Set by the compose file for this layout; listed so nobody sets them here by mistake:
#   ATLAS_BASE_URL=https://atlas-api:9443          where atlas-mcp reaches the API
#   ATLAS_CA_BUNDLE=/run/secrets/atlas_ca          the CA atlas-mcp trusts (= ./secrets/tls/ca.crt)
#   ATLAS_INTERNAL_HOSTS=atlas-api:9443            the API admits atlas-mcp's Bearer there only
#   ATLAS_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=<ATLAS_ORIGIN_SUBNET> whose CF-Connecting-IP the API believes
#   ATLAS_CERT_EXPIRY_FILE=/run/secrets/tls_cert   the certificate /api/health watches (= ./secrets/tls/server.crt)

# Certificate expiry (TASK-1419). /api/health lists the server certificate under
# `cert_warnings` once it is this many days from expiry (1-3650, default 30), and when it has
# expired. init-certs.sh issues it for 397 days; renew with `init-certs.sh --force`
# (docs/oss/guides/operations.md, section 5, "The expiry warning").
# ATLAS_CERT_EXPIRY_WARN_DAYS=30

# ============================================================================================
# Secrets: files only
# ============================================================================================

# Refuse any secret given as a plain environment variable (atlas-api and atlas-mcp).
ATLAS_REQUIRE_SECRET_FILES=1

# Set by the compose file to files init-secrets.sh writes; never put a value here:
#   ATLAS_DB_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/db_url                 (the DSN, with the DB password)
#   ATLAS_PASSWORD_PEPPER_FILE=/run/secrets/password_pepper
#   ATLAS_TOKEN_PEPPER_FILE=/run/secrets/token_pepper
# The plain forms ATLAS_DB_URL, ATLAS_PASSWORD_PEPPER and ATLAS_TOKEN_PEPPER are refused above.
# The peppers are mixed into every password verifier and token digest: regenerating them
# signs everyone out for good. Keep ./secrets/ backed up with the database.

# An external or managed PostgreSQL instead of atlas-postgres: the overlay external-db.yml
# (docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f external-db.yml, or COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:external-db.yml).
# The URL stays in ./secrets/db_url; docs/oss/guides/external-postgres.md has the steps.
# The HOST file external-db.yml makes the db_ca secret from: the database server's CA, mounted
# at /run/secrets/db_ca, which the URL names (?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/run/secrets/db_ca).
# Read by the compose file only. Unset: /dev/null, an empty mount nothing reads (sslmode=require).
# ATLAS_DB_CA_SOURCE=./secrets/db-ca.crt

# ============================================================================================
# What the install contains, and who sees what (ADR 0041)
# ============================================================================================

# What an EMPTY database is seeded with: none (an empty instance), demo (one neutral example
# project) or self (Atlas's own world model: its parts, their documents and a reviewed set of
# intents, with no work items; regenerated by tools/export_self_seed.py). Read once, at the
# first start on an empty graph. The code default, homelab, is the personal tree.
ATLAS_SEED=none
# The frozen v0 tables and /api/tree, /api/summary, /api/priority, /api/goals: off.
ATLAS_LEGACY_V0=0

# The handbook at /docs (the documents of the atlas-system node, which none and demo both seed)
# readable without signing in: static pages only, the same text as the public repository
# (ADR 0047). 0, or unset, keeps /docs behind sign-in.
ATLAS_PUBLIC_DOCS=1

# Which public landing page "/" shows a visitor who is not signed in: v1 (today's short page) or
# v2 (the product website, first-party script under a strict Content-Security-Policy, ADR 0049).
# Unset = v1, and nothing else becomes public. v2 makes the website's own stylesheet, script and
# images readable without signing in, exact paths only.
# ATLAS_LANDING=v2

# The setup guide (an owner runs it through the atlas_setup_guide MCP tool) asks the shipped
# question set. To ask your own topics as well, name a JSON file of the same shape, mounted
# into atlas-api; its topics come after the shipped ones. Unset = the shipped set only.
# ATLAS_SETUP_QUESTIONS_FILE=/run/config/setup_questions.json

# Project-scoped RBAC: roles on projects, private by default, shared projects read-only for
# everyone else, and an instance admin is not an implicit owner of every project.
ATLAS_RBAC=projects
ATLAS_DEFAULT_PROJECT_VISIBILITY=private
ATLAS_SHARED_PROJECT_ROLE=viewer
ATLAS_INSTANCE_ADMIN_IMPLICIT_PROJECT_OWNER=false

# ============================================================================================
# Sign-in: OIDC single sign-on and IdP groups (ADR 0041 §5-§7, STORY-oss-13)
# ============================================================================================
# Commented: the work install's values, to set once Atlas is registered as a confidential
# client (authorization code + PKCE) in the IdP, with redirect URI
# https://ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST/auth/oidc/callback. Unset, sign-in is local accounts only.
#
# Providers: local | oidc | local,oidc. With oidc only, the login page shows "Sign in with SSO".
# ATLAS_AUTH_PROVIDERS=oidc
# Password login: all (every local account) | break-glass (only accounts made with
# `atlas-admin user add <name> --role owner --break-glass`, at the unlinked
# /auth/login?break_glass=1; every use is a `login_break_glass` auth_event). Keep the
# break-glass credential in the enterprise vault: it is how you get in when the IdP is down.
# ATLAS_LOCAL_LOGIN=break-glass
#
# The issuer (discovery is read from <issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration) and the client.
# Entra ID: https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0 with ATLAS_OIDC_PRESET=entra
# (a tenant id, never common/organizations). Keycloak: https://<host>/realms/<realm> with
# ATLAS_OIDC_PRESET=keycloak.
# ATLAS_OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
# ATLAS_OIDC_PRESET=entra
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<application (client) id>
# How Atlas authenticates at the token endpoint (client_secret_basic, client_secret_post or
# private_key_jwt), and the client credential, which is a FILE (the plain variable is refused).
# The overlay oidc.yml
# (docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f oidc.yml) mounts ./secrets/oidc_client_secret at
# /run/secrets/oidc_client_secret, and gives atlas-api its route to the IdP (`egress`).
# ATLAS_OIDC_TOKEN_AUTH=client_secret_basic
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/oidc_client_secret
# Or a key instead of a secret (Entra: a certificate credential; KEY_ID is its base64url
# SHA-1 thumbprint, sent as x5t). oidc.yml mounts it at /run/secrets/oidc_client_key once
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_KEY_SOURCE below names the file:
# ATLAS_OIDC_TOKEN_AUTH=private_key_jwt
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/oidc_client_key
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_KEY_ID=
#
# The HOST file oidc.yml makes the oidc_client_secret secret from (read by the compose file
# only). Unset: ./secrets/oidc_client_secret. With private_key_jwt there is no secret: /dev/null.
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET_SOURCE=./secrets/oidc_client_secret
#
# The HOST file oidc.yml makes the oidc_client_key secret from (read by the compose file only).
# Unset: /dev/null, an empty mount nothing reads. With private_key_jwt, the key's file.
# ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_KEY_SOURCE=./secrets/oidc_client_key
#
# The HOST file oidc.yml makes the oidc_group_map secret from (read by the compose file only).
# Unset: ./secrets/oidc_group_map.yml. With no group map: /dev/null, and leave
# ATLAS_OIDC_GROUP_MAP_FILE unset.
# ATLAS_OIDC_GROUP_MAP_SOURCE=./secrets/oidc_group_map.yml
#
# The callback URL sent to the IdP; it must equal the redirect URI registered there. Derived
# from ATLAS_PUBLIC_URL while unset.
# ATLAS_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://atlas.example.com/auth/oidc/callback
#
# The scopes requested at the authorization endpoint, space-separated; openid is required, and
# a list without it stops atlas-api at boot.
# ATLAS_OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email
# A preset's claim names, overridden one at a time (e.g. groups from `roles`).
# ATLAS_OIDC_NAME_CLAIM=
# ATLAS_OIDC_EMAIL_CLAIM=
# ATLAS_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=
#
# Groups. Only the IdP groups listed in the map file reach Atlas (a YAML `groups:` mapping of
# IdP group value -> Atlas group name; Entra: the group's object id, Keycloak: its path), and a
# user's IdP memberships are replaced from the token at every login. Entra omits the groups
# claim past ~200 groups ("overage") and Atlas then REFUSES the login: configure the app
# registration to emit only "Groups assigned to the application".
# ATLAS_OIDC_GROUP_MAP_FILE=/run/secrets/oidc_group_map
# Accounts are created at first login (as member) only for someone in one of these IdP
# groups, and every login needs one. Unset = no automatic accounts at all.
# ATLAS_OIDC_REQUIRED_GROUPS=<atlas-users group object id>
#
# Offboarding: an SSO session lasts at most this long, and a person's agent tokens stop
# working this many days after their last SSO login (until they sign in again).
# ATLAS_OIDC_MAX_SESSION_HOURS=12
# ATLAS_OIDC_REVALIDATE_DAYS=30
#
# Agent credentials for SSO people (TASK-1415). Creating one under Settings, Agent credentials,
# asks for the person's password again, and an SSO account has none. With step-up on, an SSO
# person confirms with a FRESH sign-in at the IdP instead (prompt=login, max_age), and the
# credential is created only if the ID token's auth_time is at most MAX_AGE seconds old.
# Local accounts (break-glass) keep the password prompt. Off: today, password for everyone.
# Entra ID: add the optional claim auth_time to the ID token in the app registration.
# ATLAS_OIDC_STEP_UP=on
# ATLAS_OIDC_STEP_UP_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=300
#
# An existing local account moves to SSO, keeping its history and agents, with
#   atlas-admin user link-idp <username> --subject <the IdP's id for them>
# and an operator mints an agent token for a person (written only to the file) with
#   atlas-admin token mint --user <person> --purpose <purpose> --out <path>

# ============================================================================================
# MCP clients with an IdP access token (ADR 0041 §9, STORY-oss-25)
# ============================================================================================
# Commented: an MCP client (Claude Code) may authenticate with an OAuth access token from the
# IdP above instead of a minted atlas_svc_ token (which keeps working). Needs ATLAS_AUTH_PROVIDERS
# to include oidc, and the person to have signed in to the board with SSO once (no account is
# created from a token). atlas-mcp then serves /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource and its
# 401s point there; atlas-api checks the token (issuer, signature, audience, expiry) and acts
# as an agent account per (person, OAuth client), linked to the person. Unset: today.
#
# The resource: the MCP endpoint as clients reach it. The token's `aud` must carry it.
# That is the main door's /mcp, as written in the client's URL: add :ATLAS_TLS_PORT only when the
# port is not 443 (it is compared as written, so :443 here and none in the client's URL differ).
# ATLAS_OAUTH_RESOURCE=https://atlas.example.com/mcp
# Further accepted audiences. Entra v2 access tokens carry the resource app's client id (the
# app registration that exposes the API, never ATLAS_OIDC_CLIENT_ID), not the URI:
# ATLAS_OAUTH_AUDIENCES=<resource app (client) id>
# IdP scope -> Atlas tier (read | write | admin); the highest wins, nothing mapped is read-only,
# and the person's role and project roles still cap it.
# ATLAS_OAUTH_SCOPE_MAP=Atlas.Read=read,Atlas.Write=write
# The scopes a client should request, verbatim as the IdP names them (sent in the metadata and
# the 401 challenge). Entra: the full api://<app>/<scope> form.
# ATLAS_MCP_OAUTH_SCOPES=api://<resource app id>/Atlas.Write

# The personal/homelab features, all off: GitHub linking, the sign-off tool, the deploy watch
# and its warnings, attention probes.
ATLAS_DISABLED_FEATURES=github_link,save_work,deploy_status,deploy_warning,attention_probes

# The breached-password check against the bundled list, with no outbound call (the API has no
# route out in this layout).
ATLAS_BREACH_CHECK=bundled

# Whether an agent may record acceptance on a workitem: off (default) | attested.
# ATLAS_ACCEPTANCE_AGENT_WRITE=off

# Strict API: a field POST/PATCH /api/node or /api/workitem would ignore (unknown, or not
# applied) is a 422 naming it, instead of a `warnings` entry on an accepted write (the default).
ATLAS_STRICT_API=1

# Configuration changes from the board: 1 lets an owner, in a browser session and with their
# password each time, change the few settings marked changeable on the Configuration tab
# (today ATLAS_STRICT_API and ATLAS_RETRIEVAL_TOP_K). A variable set in this file always wins
# over the board, so a setting assigned here cannot be changed there. 0, or unset, is read-only.
# An SSO-only owner has no password: with ATLAS_OIDC_STEP_UP=on they confirm each change with a
# fresh sign-in at the identity provider instead.
# ATLAS_CONFIG_WRITES=0

# ============================================================================================
# atlas-mcp
# ============================================================================================

# Offer atlas_delete. Grants nothing: each call still needs the caller's own admin scope.
# ATLAS_MCP_ENABLE_DELETE=1
# How long the door remembers that the API accepted a token, in seconds.
# ATLAS_MCP_AUTH_CACHE_S=60
# The shared-token migration fallback is for the homelab's history only. Leave both unset:
# every caller sends its own Bearer token. (ATLAS_TOKEN would be a secret: ATLAS_TOKEN_FILE.)
# ATLAS_MCP_ALLOW_SHARED_TOKEN=
# ATLAS_TOKEN_FILE=

# ============================================================================================
# The MCP gateway: the optional `gateway` profile (STORY-oss-15)
# ============================================================================================
# AgentGateway in front of atlas-mcp (and any other MCP server listed in
# ../gateway/gateway.yaml), on its own HTTPS port with the install's certificate. Off unless the
# profile is started. To turn it on, in this directory:
#
#   python3 ../../scripts/render-gateway.py    # writes ./gateway/agentgateway.yaml from
#                                              # ../gateway/gateway.yaml, ATLAS_PUBLIC_URL (its
#                                              # CORS origin) and ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST
#   docker compose --profile gateway up -d     # or set COMPOSE_PROFILES=gateway below
#
# Agents connect to https://ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST:ATLAS_GATEWAY_PORT/mcp with their own token. The
# gateway forwards that token to atlas-mcp over HTTPS (verified against ./secrets/tls/ca.crt),
# keeps tool names as they are (atlas_get), and allows no CORS origin but the ones named.
# Re-run render-gateway.py after changing ATLAS_PUBLIC_URL, ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOST or the server
# list, then `docker compose --profile gateway up -d --force-recreate atlas-gateway`.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=gateway
# The port and interface of the gateway's TLS door.
ATLAS_GATEWAY_PORT=8444
ATLAS_GATEWAY_BIND=0.0.0.0
# MCP session-ID encryption through the gateway (TASK-1460): off unless this names a key file.
# Off, the gateway's MCP session IDs are only base64-encoded (a session ID is a routing handle,
# not a credential: every call still carries its own token). On, they are AES-256-GCM encrypted
# with a 32-byte key, kept in a file you create, 0600 and owned by you:
#   `( umask 077; openssl rand -hex 32 > ./secrets/gateway_session_key )`
# render-gateway.py reads this setting (not docker compose): it reads the key from the file
# (a path relative to this directory, or absolute) and writes it into ./gateway/agentgateway.yaml,
# which it then writes 0600 (AgentGateway v1.7.0 takes the key only inline in its config or as an
# environment variable). The key itself never goes in this file. Render again and recreate
# atlas-gateway after setting, changing or unsetting it; a new key ends every open session.
# ATLAS_GATEWAY_SESSION_KEY_FILE=./secrets/gateway_session_key

# ============================================================================================
# Retrieval: the optional semantic-retrieval add-on (ADR 0044). Off unless you turn it on.
# ============================================================================================
# The base install has keyword search only. To turn the add-on on, set the block below and start
# with the overlay: `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f retrieval.yml up -d` (it gives
# atlas-api its route out and the key as the secret ./secrets/embed_key). It runs inside
# atlas-api and reaches embeddings only through this OpenAI-compatible endpoint; no model ships
# in any image. A broken configuration never stops Atlas: /api/health says
# `retrieval: unavailable: <reason>`, and search stays full-text.
# ATLAS_RETRIEVAL=off
# https:// is required (plain http only to loopback). A `/v1` base gets `/embeddings` appended;
# a URL ending in /embeddings is used as given, query string included.
# ATLAS_EMBED_URL=https://<your-endpoint>/openai/deployments/<deployment>/embeddings?api-version=2024-10-21
#
# The model name, sent as `model` on every call and stored on every indexed row; changing it
# re-embeds everything (vectors of two models are never mixed).
# ATLAS_EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
#
# The vector length the endpoint returns for that model, and the size of the vector column (at
# most 16000). A vector of another length is refused; changing it on an index that holds vectors
# needs POST /api/retrieval/reindex with rebuild=true.
# ATLAS_EMBED_DIM=1536
#
# The PATH of the file holding the endpoint's key, read on every call (a rotated key needs no
# restart); unset for an endpoint that takes no key, and in entra mode. retrieval.yml mounts
# ./secrets/embed_key there. The key is only ever a file: a plain ATLAS_EMBED_KEY variable makes
# the add-on unavailable.
# ATLAS_EMBED_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/embed_key
#
# The HOST file retrieval.yml makes the embed_key secret from (read by the compose file only).
# Unset: ./secrets/embed_key. In entra mode set /dev/null, so no key file has to exist.
# ATLAS_EMBED_KEY_SOURCE=/dev/null
#
# bearer (Authorization: Bearer with the key), api-key (Azure OpenAI's header), or entra: an Entra
# ID token from the workload identity (AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE, AZURE_CLIENT_ID,
# AZURE_TENANT_ID; the overlay retrieval-workload-identity.yml mounts the token) or managed
# identity the container runs as. entra takes no key file.
# ATLAS_EMBED_AUTH_HEADER=bearer
# The token scope in entra mode (a sovereign cloud has its own).
# ATLAS_EMBED_ENTRA_SCOPE=https://cognitiveservices.azure.com/.default
# A PEM bundle for an endpoint whose certificate is from a private CA (e.g. /run/secrets/atlas_ca).
# ATLAS_EMBED_CA_BUNDLE=
# shared never sends the text of a private project to the endpoint; all sends everything.
# ATLAS_EMBED_SCOPE=shared
#
# Text put verbatim before every indexed chunk, for a model trained with one (nomic-embed-text:
# "search_document: ", trailing space included). Empty by default; changing it re-embeds.
# ATLAS_EMBED_DOC_PREFIX=
#
# Text put verbatim before every search query, the query side of the same convention (nomic:
# "search_query: "). Empty by default.
# ATLAS_EMBED_QUERY_PREFIX=
# Texts per embeddings call, and seconds between index passes.
# ATLAS_EMBED_BATCH=32
# ATLAS_RETRIEVAL_INTERVAL_S=60
# How many nodes the vector list contributes to a mode=semantic|hybrid search (1-200).
# ATLAS_RETRIEVAL_TOP_K=30

# ============================================================================================
# Leave unset on an install (listed because the code reads them)
# ============================================================================================
# The deploy commands a stale-deploy warning names (off here: deploy_status, deploy_warning).
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_HINT_HOST=
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_HINT_REBUILD_API=
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_HINT_REBUILD_MCP=
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_HINT_PULL_REBUILD_API=
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_HINT_PULL_REBUILD_MCP=
# Where the deploy watch reads the source tree and its status file (homelab only).
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_SRC=
# ATLAS_DEPLOY_WATCH_FILE=
# GitHub linking (off here: github_link). The three credentials are files only.
# ATLAS_GITHUB_REPO=
# ATLAS_GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID_FILE=
# ATLAS_GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
# ATLAS_GITHUB_INVITE_TOKEN_FILE=
# `admin session mint` (a browser session with no password): staging only, never an install.
# ATLAS_ALLOW_SESSION_MINT=
# A staged-rollout bypass for unauthenticated machine callers: never on an install.
# ATLAS_MACHINE_GRACE_HOSTS=
# Wipes the graph at startup (development only). Never on an install.
# ATLAS_RESEED=
# The operator name atlas-admin records (defaults to the shell user).
# ATLAS_ADMIN_ACTOR=
# Serve the board from another directory (development only).
# ATLAS_FRONTEND_DIR=

# ============================================================================================
# Read audit and audit export (STORY-oss-16). Unset = today: no read is logged, nothing purged.
# With ATLAS_AUDIT_READS=1 every agent (service-token) read of a node, a search or a document
# writes an audit_read row (person, agent, token id, MCP tool, node ids, request id).
# GET /api/audit/export streams graph_event, auth_event and audit_read as JSON Lines to an
# owner, or to an owner's agent token minted with `read,admin`.
ATLAS_AUDIT_READS=1
# Days of audit_read kept. Enforced by `atlas-admin audit purge`, which you schedule, e.g.
#   0 3 * * *  docker compose exec -T atlas-api atlas-admin audit purge
# Only audit_read is purged; graph_event and auth_event are kept forever.
ATLAS_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# ============================================================================================
# Backups: routine schedule, retention, host directory (OSS-26 C). Unset = no routine backup.
# ============================================================================================
# The `backup` profile (docker compose --profile backup up -d, or COMPOSE_PROFILES=backup)
# runs the backup loop. Without the variables below it acts only during a crunch week whose
# backup policy asks for it. Every backup is pg_dump, a sha256 and a manifest, restored into a
# temporary database and counted before it is kept. Restoring one (atlas-api stopped first) is
# `docker compose run --rm --no-deps atlas-backup atlas-admin backup restore latest`, and
# docs/oss/guides/operations.md has the whole procedure, the dry run and the rollback.
#
# A routine backup outside crunch week: off (default), hourly, daily or weekly. The crunch
# policy is still asked first; a routine backup is taken only when the crunch took none.
# ATLAS_BACKUP_SCHEDULE=daily
# How many routine backups to keep (1-1000, default 7). After each verified routine backup the
# older routine backups are deleted; manual, per-deploy and crunch backups are never deleted by it.
# ATLAS_BACKUP_KEEP=7
# An absolute host directory for the backup files instead of the `atlas-backups` volume, so
# they can be copied off this host. Create it first, writable by the container's group 0
# (`sudo install -d -m 0770 -g 0 /srv/atlas-backups`).
# ATLAS_BACKUP_HOST_DIR=/srv/atlas-backups

# ============================================================================================
# Released images (OSS-26 E). Unset = the default: every image is built from this checkout.
# ============================================================================================
# Only with the overlay release.yml (docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f release.yml, or
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:release.yml), which replaces each local build with the image
# a release published to the GitHub container registry. Every one is required by that overlay,
# and each is pinned by tag AND digest: a tag can be moved, a digest cannot. Copy the five lines
# from the release's `release-images.env` asset (or docs/oss/RELEASES.md), keep this checkout at
# the same release tag, and replace all five together on every upgrade. Without release.yml
# these lines are read by nothing.
#
# atlas-api, atlas-migrate and the retrieval check (backend/Dockerfile, target hardened):
# ATLAS_IMAGE_API=ghcr.io/<owner>/gradinai-atlas-api:v0.0.1-alpha@sha256:<digest>
# atlas-mcp (mcp/Dockerfile, target hardened):
# ATLAS_IMAGE_MCP=ghcr.io/<owner>/gradinai-atlas-mcp:v0.0.1-alpha@sha256:<digest>
# atlas-backup, the backup profile (backend/Dockerfile, target backup: hardened plus pg_dump 16):
# ATLAS_IMAGE_BACKUP=ghcr.io/<owner>/gradinai-atlas-backup:v0.0.1-alpha@sha256:<digest>
# atlas-postgres (deploy/postgres/pgvector.Dockerfile: pgvector on PostgreSQL 16, gosu replaced):
# ATLAS_IMAGE_POSTGRES=ghcr.io/<owner>/gradinai-atlas-postgres:v0.0.1-alpha@sha256:<digest>
# atlas-tls, the TLS ingress (deploy/caddy/Dockerfile: Caddy with the Cloudflare DNS plugin):
# ATLAS_IMAGE_TLS=ghcr.io/<owner>/gradinai-atlas-tls:v0.0.1-alpha@sha256:<digest>
